WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.350
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:09.450
Neural networks are amazing at recognizing
objects when being shown an image, and in

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.969
some cases, like traffic sign recognition,
their performance can reach superhuman levels.

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:18.760
But as we discussed in the previous episode,
most of these networks have an interesting

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:23.820
property where we can add small changes to
an input photo and have the network misclassify

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.540
it to something completely different.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:26.540
This is called an adversarial attack.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:30.540
A super effective neural network can be reduced
to something that is less accurate than a

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.730
coinflip with a properly crafted adversarial
attack.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:39.040
So, of course, we may think that neural networks
are much smaller and simpler than the human

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.820
brain, and because of that, of course, we
cannot perform such an adversarial attack

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.920
on the human vision system...right?

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:52.140
Or is it possible that some of the properties
of machine vision systems can be altered to

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.199
fool the human vision?

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:58.120
And now, hold on to your papers, I think you
know what's coming.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.360
This algorithm performs an adversarial attack
on you.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.600
This image depicts a cat.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.659
And this image depicts, uh... a dog?

00:01:07.659 --> 00:01:09.520
Surely it's a dog, right?

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.049
Well, no.

00:01:11.049 --> 00:01:16.090
This is an image of a previous cat plus some
carefully crafted noise that makes it look

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.360
like a dog.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.490
This is such a peculiar effect - I am staring
at it, and I know for a fact that this is

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:27.980
not a dog, this is cat plus noise, but I cannot
not see it as a dog.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:31.770
Wow, this is certainly something that you
don't see every day.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.119
So let's look at what changes were made to
the image.

00:01:34.119 --> 00:01:39.200
Clearly, the nose appears to be longer and
thicker, so that's a dog-like feature.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.260
But, it is of utmost importance that we don't
overlook the fact that several cat-specific

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:49.420
features still remain in the image, for instance,
the whiskers are very cat-like.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:52.640
And despite that, we still see it as a dog.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:53.660
This is insanity.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:59.659
The technique works by performing an adversarial
attack against an AI model, and modifying

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:03.860
the noise generator model to better match
the human visual system.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:08.220
Of course, the noise we have to add depends
on the architecture of the neural network,

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:12.709
and by this I mean the number of layers and
the number of neurons within these layers,

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:14.150
and many other parameters.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:19.140
However, a key insight of the paper is that
there are still features that are shared between

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.660
most architectures.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.790
This means that if we create an attack that
works against 5 different neural network architectures,

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:30.800
it is highly likely that it will also work
on an arbitrary sixth network that we haven't

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.110
seen yet.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.440
And it turns out that some of these noise
distributions are also useful against the

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:37.970
human visual system.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:42.200
Make sure to have a look at the paper, I have
found it to be an easy read, and quite frankly

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:43.840
I am stunned by the result.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.910
It is clear that machine learning research
is progressing at a staggering pace, but I

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:50.160
haven't expected this.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.140
I haven't expected this at all.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.910
